<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laayoune</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as El Aaiún</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Laayoune is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, near the Atlantic coast and the seasonal river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguia-el-hamra">Saguia el-Hamra</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1938 as a military post, it grew into the capital of the colony of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the outlet for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">phosphates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bou-craa">Bou Craa</a>. Since the Spanish withdrawal in 1976 it has been administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, which treats it as a regional capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laayoune.jpg" alt="Laayoune" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collage compiled by LLs -- see constituent images for individual author information · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Aai%C3%BAn-La%C3%A2youne_Collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
